   "id": "fan-li",
   "name": "范蠡 (Fan Li)",
   "who": {
    "markdown": "范蠡 was the chief strategist and diplomat of 越王勾踐 (Goujian) of Yue, a small state on the southeastern Chinese coast that had just been crushed by Wu and reduced to a rump around Mt. Kuaiji (會稽) in the early 5th century BCE. In 國語·越語下 — a state-speech record compiled within roughly a century of his death — he is the man the king summons year after year and who keeps answering 未可也, \"not yet.\" He framed every situation through three channels he names himself: 持盈者與天、定傾者與人、節事者與地 (\"when at the full, accord with Heaven; when in danger, accord with people; when handling affairs, accord with the ground\") — 國語/卷21.史記 records that he was made 上將軍 and that he personally negotiated his own release from Wu as a hostage (質) and helped reduce 吴 in 473 BCE — 史記/卷41. After the war he refused Goujian's offer to share the kingdom, saying 「君行令，臣行意」 (\"your word is command; mine is intent\") and sailed away with his household — 史記/卷41, echoed in 國語/卷21. He then reinvented himself three times: 鴟夷子皮 farming at the sea's edge in Qi, briefly prime minister of Qi, and finally 陶朱公 (Lord Zhu of Tao) at the market-hub of Tao, where 史記 says he died of old age and 太史公 concludes 「范蠡三遷皆有榮名，名垂後世」 (\"each of Fan Li's three relocations brought him honoured name\"). His commercial method is credited to his teacher 計然 (Ji Ran) — 史記·貨殖列傳 quotes 計然 directly on buying low and selling high, and quotes 范蠡 saying 「計然之策七，越用其五而得意。既已施於國，吾欲用之家」 (\"Ji Ran's plan had seven parts; Yue used five and prevailed. Now that it has been applied to the state, I want to apply it to the household\") — so the economic doctrine is *his teacher's, executed by him*, not his own invention.",
    "source": "runs/20261013-four-seats/identity/fan-li.md"
   },
   "list_source": "runs/20261013-four-seats/final-opus/fan-li.md",
